From: =?UTF-8?q?Witold=20Gre=C5=84?= <witold.gren@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 17:49:08 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 23/25] rtsp: answer a new client at once from the stream's
 last start

A sleeping battery or 4G camera needs 10-20 s to connect and send its first frame, and a new
RTSP client was answered only after that: the pipeline was built from the camera's first
frames. go2rtc (Home Assistant's WebRTC) waits only 5 s for the answer to DESCRIBE, SETUP and
PLAY whatever #timeout= says, so a live view of a sleeping camera always failed over WebRTC and
the browser fell back to HLS (about 40 s to a picture, sound seconds behind).

Each live view now keeps the start of its stream (codecs, frame rate, the first keyframe with
its parameter sets, the first audio frame) in memory and in <snapshot_dir>/stream. The next
client gets a pipeline prerolled with that start at once (the keyframe twice, so that the
byte-stream parser lets the first one go, and a short repeat of the sound for the parser and
encoders), while the camera is woken in the background; its live video follows from its first
keyframe, and a camera whose stream changed is forgotten and started over. The media is no
longer suspended with Reset after DESCRIBE, which would have needed a new preroll on PLAY.
